Book citations (format spec §9C.4) are formatted at build time by citeproc-js (npm package citeproc 2.4.63, Copyright
(c) 2009-2019 Frank Bennett; upstream https://github.com/Juris-M/citeproc-js at commit
73bc1b44bc7d54d0bfec4e070fd27f5efe024ff9, https://github.com/Juris-M/citeproc-js/tree/73bc1b44bc7d54d0bfec4e070fd27f5efe024ff9 :
that version has no git tag, and this is the commit whose package.json says 2.4.63 and whose citeproc_commonjs.js and
LICENSE are byte-identical to the npm files; vendor/citeproc/SOURCE.json records it with their sha256). Its LICENSE offers the Common Public
Attribution License 1.0 or any later version, or the GNU Affero General Public License 3.0 or any later version
("CPAL-1.0+ OR AGPL-3.0+"); Egatari Studio uses it under the CPAL-1.0 (text: https://opensource.org/licenses/CPAL-1.0
and the file CPAL of that commit, copied unmodified to vendor/citeproc/CPAL with its Exhibits A and B). citeproc is an unmodified dependency, installed by npm and loaded by
the command-line build (kit/build.mjs, when a book cites references) and by the editor app when it shows or saves a
book that cites references. Built lessons and single files (.ega.html) carry only the formatted citation text, and
this repository and the npm package egatari do not contain or bundle citeproc. The editor app does not bundle it in
app.js: the hosted editor serves the npm file citeproc_commonjs.js of citeproc 2.4.63 byte-identical (not minified, not
transpiled), with its LICENSE, package.json, the CPAL text (Exhibits A and B) and SOURCE.json, as vendor/citeproc/ next
to the app, and loads it only for a book with references; the Offline editor (dist/editor.html) carries the same file
byte for byte, header comment included, in a <script type="text/plain" id="egatari-citeproc"> element outside the app
script, and runs it only then. The source of this Covered Code is that file itself (JavaScript as published on npm, the
same bytes as citeproc_commonjs.js at the upstream commit above). The Attribution Information of its Exhibit B is:
"(c) Frank Bennett", "citeproc-js implements the Citation Style Language", https://citationstyles.org/.
This attribution is displayed wherever citeproc runs behind something the user sees: the command-line build and
pack print it ("Citations formatted by citeproc-js — (c) Frank Bennett — citeproc-js implements the Citation Style
Language — https://citationstyles.org/") whenever citeproc formatted citations, and the editor app shows the same
line under the book and in its Book panel whenever citeproc formatted the book's citations. The dev server preview
builds the slides only and does not run citeproc. CPAL section 15 (network use): citeproc runs in the user's own
browser, and the hosted editor makes the unmodified source available as served (vendor/citeproc/); Egatari Studio
does not run builds as a hosted service.
The editor app's book tools (dist/article-tools.js, loaded only for lessons with a book) bundle myst-parser,
myst-transforms, myst-ext-proof, myst-ext-exercise (MIT, Executable Books) and their dependencies (markdown-it,
unified, js-yaml, he and others; MIT, ISC or BSD licenses), and the CSL styles and locales of vendor/csl/.
The citation styles and locales in vendor/csl/ (apa.csl, ieee.csl, sist02.csl, locales-en-US.xml, locales-ja-JP.xml) are unmodified copies
from the Citation Style Language projects (https://github.com/citation-style-language/styles and /locales; commits
in vendor/csl/SOURCE.json), licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0 License, as each file
states in its <rights> element.

abcjs 6.7.2 renders score symbols at build time (MIT). The editor bundles abcjs
for rendering only; built lessons carry the SVG and no abcjs runtime.

